LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE WORK

Limitations

Review volume is an imperfect demand proxy. It is a downstream signal of purchasing, not a direct measure of unit sales. Review propensity varies by product category, price, and time period. Actual unit sales data would be a stronger target variable.

The analysis is US-only. Google Trends was queried for the US region. The model may not generalise to other regions without retraining.

There is no social media sentiment data. The original proposal included Reddit sentiment as a third signal, but the available Reddit dataset covered only three weeks in December 2022, making it incompatible with the 2013-2018 analysis window. It was excluded.

All reviews in the two categories are treated equally regardless of price. A cheap accessory and an expensive laptop contribute identically to the weekly demand metric.

The analysis covers 2013-2018 and does not include the COVID-19 period, the global chip shortage, or the shift to remote work, all of which caused structural changes in electronics demand.

Hyperparameters were not tuned. All models used near-default settings. A proper search could improve performance by 5 to 15 percent.

Future Work

Building separate models for smartphone-related versus laptop-related keywords would better capture the distinct demand dynamics of each product type.

A formal Granger causality test would provide a stronger causal claim than the correlation-based evidence currently presented.

LSTM networks and Transformer-based time-series models could capture long-range temporal dependencies that tree-based models miss.

Collecting Reddit data from r/gadgets and r/electronics using the Pushshift historical archive for 2013-2018 would enable a fair evaluation of social media sentiment as a third signal.

The pipeline could be operationalised as a real-time demand forecasting system since Google Trends data is available daily.


